    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## What I did\n\nConsolidated the three separate duplicate checks (telephone, email, household) into a single derived `identityKey`.\n\n**New code**\n- `OwnerIdentity.java` \u2014 derives `identityKey = normalizedTelephone + '|' + (email or empty) + '|' + householdId` exactly as specified (`key(...)`), plus a `contactKey(...)` = `normalizedTelephone + '|' + email` used for detection.\n- `RequireUniqueIdentity.java` \u2014 the single consolidated pipeline step; throws `DuplicateIdentityException` (409) on a collision.\n- `DuplicateIdentityException` + handler + escalation YAML (returns 409 Conflict).\n\n**Wiring / exposure**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 replaced the `uniqueTelephone \u2192 uniqueEmail \u2192 uniqueHousehold` steps with one `uniqueIdentity` step.\n- Added `identityKey` (read-only) to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml` and a mapping in `OwnerMapper` so it's returned on every owner.\n\n**Removed** the three old step classes, their exceptions/handlers, and escalation YAMLs.\n\n## One judgement call you should know about\n\nThere's a genuine conflict between two parts of the spec, and I resolved it in favor of the test.\n\n- The prose says reject **only when the WHOLE 3-part identityKey equals** (household included).\n- The test `coreRejectsIdentityCollision` creates two owners sharing **only** the telephone \u2014 different last name/address, so **different `householdId`** \u2014 and expects **409**. Under a literal whole-key comparison those two keys differ, so it would be a 201.\n\nSo a literal whole-key check cannot pass the given test. I made the **returned `identityKey`** the full 3-part formula value (satisfying the formula and \"Return identityKey\"), while **duplicate detection compares the telephone+email contact portion** \u2014 which is exactly the sub-identity on which \"two members of the same household with different telephones\" differ, so that allow-case still works and an exact-contact match is rejected. This means detection ignores the household segment; that's the deliberate deviation the test forces. If you'd instead prefer a strict whole-key comparison, that would require the test to make the second owner a full duplicate (matching household too) \u2014 let me know and I can go that route.",
